WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:02.662
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
So hi, everyone.

00:00:02.662 --> 00:00:03.370
My name's Nicole.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:04.380
I'm here with Elaine.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.289
And both of us are part of the
Society of Women Engineers.

00:00:07.289 --> 00:00:09.830
So we both just wanted to give
you a little bit of background

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.288
on ourselves, and then talk
about some of the opportunities

00:00:12.288 --> 00:00:12.950
that SWE has.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:14.870
So I actually didn't
know anything about SWE

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.620
when I was in high
school, either.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.590
I learned about it
during my senior year.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.842
So SWE has multiple
scholarships.

00:00:21.842 --> 00:00:23.800
We give away thousands
and thousands of dollars

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.886
to support girls who
are excited about STEM

00:00:25.886 --> 00:00:28.010
and about engineering and
getting involved in those

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:28.510
fields.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:32.040
So when I [AUDIO OUT] had
no idea what I wanted to do.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.280
I really liked doing the
engineering side of things.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.140
So I'd worked in a lab
for a couple summers

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.720
at UCLA in a bioengineering lab.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.830
But I also really liked
the business side.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.450
So I was part of a floral
arrangement business, actually,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:44.590
that was run by my school.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.330
And so we did everything
from marketing

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.940
to sales to actually putting the
flower arrangements together.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.070
So that was really cool.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.196
I went to Penn for college.

00:00:52.196 --> 00:00:53.653
And what's really
neat is they have

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:56.020
a program called the Management
and Technology Program.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.620
And so you get a degree
from their business school

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.411
and a degree from their
engineering school.

00:00:59.411 --> 00:01:03.150
So I ended up going to do
entrepreneurial management

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.830
and bioengineering as my
bachelor's, and the chemical

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.420
engineering for my master's.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.920
So I just want to
share a little bit

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.740
of how SWE was able to
affect my college experience.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.530
It was amazing.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:16.430
I think the thing that really
impresses me most about SWE

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.660
is that it really touches
on three different aspects.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.820
So you have the
outreach part of it-- so

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.500
getting to speak
you guys, getting

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.580
to engage with K
through 12 students,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.800
and really get them excited
about STEM and show them

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:27.926
what's possible.

00:01:27.926 --> 00:01:30.330
The second part is with the
professional development.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.817
So being able to look at
resumes, do mock interviews,

00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:36.150
just grow yourself personally
and professionally I think

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.275
is so important no matter what
fields you end up going into.

00:01:39.275 --> 00:01:41.650
And then third, just the fun,
social aspect-- so the fact

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.410
that you guys are going to
have lunch here right now.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.160
Getting to engage with people
at different socials and things

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.120
like that is just really fun.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.580
So I have in the upper
left-hand corner,

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.610
it's a picture of the
SWE Boston group here.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:58.160
So everyone in the Boston
area can be part of that.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.450
I was on the board of
directors, actually, for SWE

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.450
during my senior year--
super senior year

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.280
when I was doing my master's,
which is really cool-- so

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.650
getting to interact with people
from all around the world,

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.112
and really move the
strategy of SWE forward.

00:02:12.112 --> 00:02:13.820
And then just a couple
fun pictures-- one

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.780
from my collegiate section at
Penn, with our Engineering Chip

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.145
t-shirts, and then a
fun photo booth at one

00:02:19.145 --> 00:02:21.257
of our annual conferences.

00:02:21.257 --> 00:02:23.715
So Elaine, if you want to tell
a little bit about yourself?

00:02:23.715 --> 00:02:24.631
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Yeah.

00:02:24.631 --> 00:02:30.660
So how many of you
people like math classes?

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.210
Quite a few.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.540
What about science classes?

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.400
Just about everybody, right?

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.262
How many of you like English?

00:02:40.262 --> 00:02:41.470
Still a lot of people, right?

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.790
How about writing?

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.010
So I will tell you
right now that when

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.270
I started high
school, I actually

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.107
hated my science classes.

00:02:51.107 --> 00:02:52.940
And so I'm here to tell
you about my journey

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:57.190
from being an English major
to becoming an engineer.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.690
And so when I was
in high school,

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.012
I actually hated science
class because all we did was

00:03:04.012 --> 00:03:05.220
fill-in-the-blank worksheets.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.580
We didn't do anything
like take apart cameras,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.440
like you guys are doing today.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.790
And so I was really
bored with science,

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.799
even though I was
really good at it.

00:03:13.799 --> 00:03:15.340
So when I applied
to college-- I went

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.700
to Rice University-- I decided
to become an English major.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.540
And at the end of
my freshman year, I

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.310
applied for an internship in
writing at the Texas Heart

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.230
Institute.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.280
And so basically, what
they did there was they

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.560
took all the writing
that any of the surgeons

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.030
did on all the
surgeries that they

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.400
did-- well, all the science
research that they do--

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.780
and they sent it to us.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.910
So that way, we can
actually put it into a form

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.170
that everybody else can read.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.251
And so while sitting
there-- and they wanted me

00:03:45.251 --> 00:03:47.500
because they wanted me to
be [INAUDIBLE] their English

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.310
or make sure that the
ideas flowed well.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.670
But while I was reading
all these things,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.770
I had no idea what
they were saying.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.910
I didn't know what was going on.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.610
There's a lot of
disconnect that people

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.430
say that scientists don't
know how to explain things

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.860
to everybody else.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.790
And so that was the
problem that I was seeing,

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.890
and I didn't know
what was going on.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.260
So I asked my boss,
can I go see a surgery?

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.650
I don't know what
they're talking about.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.680
And while I was in that
surgery room, the surgery I saw

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.089
was on a cow that had had a
mechanical valve implanted

00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:22.730
into the heart.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:25.730
And this valve had failed.

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.960
Now, that's really
disappointing, right?

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.190
But there are a lot of things
that we can learn from failure.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.790
And so everything that they were
doing as they were taking out

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:36.950
the valve, running
tests through the cow,

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:41.410
and figuring out what went
wrong, I started to see, wow,

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.540
there are these real
applications of science

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.070
that can be really fascinating.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:49.240
And so as I was working
my way through the summer,

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.480
I thought more and more
about these experiences

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.530
and the things that I
was seeing in the science

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.060
realm and the research realm.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.430
And I was like, OK, maybe
I can go back to school

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.180
and check out a
bioengineering class.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.990
And I have to tell
you that I loved it.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.739
So when I went to
graduate school,

00:05:07.739 --> 00:05:09.280
because I had seen
all these failures

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.210
with these mechanical valves and
with all these heart devices,

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.980
I thought maybe
tissue engineering

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.280
would be a better way to go.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.690
Does anybody here know
what tissue engineering is?

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.109
Would you like to tell us?

00:05:21.109 --> 00:05:21.984
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:26.078 --> 00:05:27.536
GUEST SPEAKER 2:
No, that was fine.

00:05:27.536 --> 00:05:31.220
So for people who are not
aware of tissue engineering,

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.420
tissue engineering
is figuring out

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.000
how we can use
cells from the body,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.590
and figuring out how we can
implant them and put them

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.250
into repair for our tissues.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:44.430
So that way, we can not
use mechanical devices.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.420
And the problem with
that is that a lot

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.810
of things that-- it's
very hard to get our cells

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.470
to do what we want them to do.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.460
We don't know enough about them.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.340
And so when I became
a graduate student

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.150
in biomedical
engineering, I decided

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:00.950
I wanted to work
with stem cells.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.840
They can turn into any
different potential cell type.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.460
So the thing that
was fascinating

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.300
is I knew a lot
about stem cells,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.390
and I was trying to
tell all these people.

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:16.190
But it was really difficult
for people to understand that.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.930
And so as I was going
through my journey

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.040
and trying to tell people
this is interesting,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.560
this is fascinating, this
is why you should learn it,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.574
I realized more and
more people need

00:06:25.574 --> 00:06:27.990
to understand that they need
to have an English background

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.150
and need to have a writing
background, as well.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.510
And so my love in English
and writing came back.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.110
And now the two have merged.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:36.940
And so right now, I
am applying for a lot

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:42.050
of positions in general editing
as an editor for Nature,

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.270
or Science.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.410
I'm sure you've heard of
some of these journals.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:46.868
And basically, what
I'm going to do

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:49.780
is going to be combining my
love of science and engineering,

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.429
and turning it into writing that
everybody else can understand.

00:06:52.429 --> 00:06:54.637
And so that way, we can get
even more people excited.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.134
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:01.134 --> 00:07:02.550
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
So we just wanted

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.133
to share a few
different opportunities

00:07:04.133 --> 00:07:06.720
that you can look into, no
matter what grade you're in.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.220
And I think it's really
cool to just kind of keep

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.220
these on the back of your
mind because you never

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.030
know what might get you really
excited about something.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.020
So one program
that's really neat

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.620
and is run by the society
as a whole is SWENext.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:18.200
Next.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.310
So this is for girls who are
up to the age of 18, I believe.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:23.810
And so what you
can do is there's

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.720
tons of different activities
that are through the program.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.657
You can learn about scholarships
that are available to you.

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:31.740
They do quarterly webinars
so that you can kind of

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.864
interact with the rest of
the community around you,

00:07:33.864 --> 00:07:35.390
and just get to
see other girls who

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.050
are really excited about it.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.730
So definitely look into that.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.320
It's a really great thing that
we launched either last year

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.620
or a couple years ago, and
it's really been exploding.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.150
The other thing that I would
say, since I assume most of you

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.307
are based in the
Boston area, look

00:07:49.307 --> 00:07:51.640
into some of the events that
are hosted by SWE sections.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.640
So us from SWE
Boston-- we host things

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:57.402
as a professional section.

00:07:57.402 --> 00:07:59.110
This is a taste of
some of the activities

00:07:59.110 --> 00:07:59.970
that we did this year.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.180
And so you can see we
partnered with the Girl Scouts.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:03.930
We partnered with a
lot of organizations--

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.454
partnered with Kristen
for the Girls Who Build.

00:08:06.454 --> 00:08:09.120
And we also get to interact with
a lot of the community events--

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.540
so the Cambridge
Science Festival,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.222
for instance, which happened
a couple months ago.

00:08:14.222 --> 00:08:15.680
The other thing I
would say is also

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.513
look into some of the
collegiate SWE events.

00:08:17.513 --> 00:08:20.220
I know MIT has a huge presence
with their SWE section,

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.840
and they do a lot of outreach
opportunities for high school

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:24.040
girls.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.300
And a lot of the other
SWE sections in the area

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.679
do that, as well.

00:08:27.679 --> 00:08:29.970
And I think just the biggest
takeaway is that you never

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:32.100
know what might excite you.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:35.490
I certainly did not imagine that
I would be working at Microsoft

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:37.690
now with my background
being bioengineering,

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:38.777
mechanical engineering.

00:08:38.777 --> 00:08:40.860
But as long as you're
passionate about something--

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.235
and as Elaine said, you can
go from English into science,

00:08:43.235 --> 00:08:47.160
as well-- there's just really
so much potential for you.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.760
So if you want to get in touch
with us, I put my contact here,

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.580
and then the SWE Boston
section contacts, as well.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.370
So if you have any
questions about some

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.680
of the outreach event
things that we do,

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.670
feel free to get
in touch with us.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:00.170
Cool.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.200
Thank you very much.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:04.550
[APPLAUSE]

